tel 1.0

base gnossienne
import soundness

info   typed references to entities, holding only their keys and resolved back through a store
detail
    _Gnossienne_ represents a reference to an entity — a person, a product, a row — as a
    value which holds nothing but the entity's key, typed by what it refers to. A
    `Reference to Person` built from `t"juliet@example.com"` is not a `Text`, and cannot be
    confused with a reference to anything else.

    How a key finds its entity is a `Resolvable` typeclass instance. The usual one is built
    from a set of values with `Resolvable(store)`, and searches them by the field marked with
    the `@primary` annotation. With that instance in scope, the `ref` extension takes the
    reference of any value, and applying a reference resolves it back, raising a
    `Reference.Error` if nothing in the store has that key.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    case class Person(@primary email: Text, name: Text)

    val romeo = Person(t"romeo@example.com", t"Romeo")
    val juliet = Person(t"juliet@example.com", t"Juliet")

    given Person is Resolvable by Text = Resolvable(Set(romeo, juliet))

    val reference: Reference to Person = juliet.ref
    reference.key                                   // t"juliet@example.com"
    reference()                                     // juliet
    safely(Reference[Person](t"nobody@example.com")())   // Unset
    ```

    A reference encodes as its key and decodes from it, so it can be stored or sent as text
    and resolved later. It is validated only when it is applied, never when it is built.

    The library has a single source set, `gnossienne.core`, for every platform, and
    everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella. The database tutorial shows
    references used together with relations.

refer gnossienne.Reference
refer gnossienne.Resolvable
refer gnossienne.primary
refer gnossienne.ref

entry .Reference   a typed reference to an entity, holding its key and resolved through a `Resolvable`
  detail
      A `Reference` holds the key of an entity and, as its `#Result` type member, the type
      of the entity it refers to: `Reference to Person` is a reference to a `Person`. It is
      made from a value with the `ref` extension, or from a key with `Reference[Person](key)`;
      either way, its `#key` is typed as the `Operand` of the `Resolvable` instance in scope.

      Applying a reference, `reference()`, looks the key up through `Person is Resolvable`
      and returns the entity, raising `Reference.Error` if there is none. A reference made
      from a key which matches nothing is still a valid reference: the failure comes only
      when it is applied.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Product(@primary code: Int, description: Text)

      val widget = Product(1001, t"Widget")
      given Product is Resolvable by Int = Resolvable(Set(widget))

      val reference = Reference[Product](1001)
      reference().description   // t"Widget"
      ```

      A reference encodes as text through `.encodable` and decodes through `.decodable`,
      each by way of the key's own codec, so `t"1001".as[Reference to Product]` reads one.
  refer gnossienne.Resolvable
  refer gnossienne.ref
  entry .apply   makes a reference from a key, as `Reference[Entity](key)`
      The key's type is the `Operand` of the `Resolvable` instance for the entity, which must
      be in scope. Nothing is looked up: a key which matches no entity makes a reference
      which raises `Reference.Error` when it is applied.
  entry .encodable   encodes a reference as text: the encoding of its key
      Available for `Reference to Entity` whenever `Entity is Resolvable` and its key type
      is `Encodable in Text`. It is in implicit scope.
  entry .decodable   decodes a reference from text, by decoding its key
      Available for `Reference to Entity` whenever `Entity is Resolvable` and its key type
      is `Decodable in Text`. The decoded reference is not checked against the store until
      it is applied.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Person(@primary email: Text, name: Text)
      given Person is Resolvable by Text = Resolvable(Set(Person(t"juliet@example.com", t"Juliet")))

      t"juliet@example.com".as[Reference to Person]().name   // t"Juliet"
      ```
  entry .Error   raised when a reference's key matches no entity in the store
    detail
        Error 519: _the reference `reference` could not be resolved because `reason`_.
        Raised by `Reference#apply` (and `Resolvable#resolve`) when the store has no entity
        whose `@primary` field equals the key. `#reference` is the key, encoded as text.
    entry #reference   the key which could not be resolved, encoded as text
    entry #reason   why the reference could not be resolved, a `Reference.Error.Reason`
    entry .Reason   why a reference could not be resolved
        The only case is `NotFound`: _no target with that reference was found in the
        store_.
    entry .communicable   renders a `Reference.Error.Reason` as a message
  entry #Result   the type of the entity the reference refers to
  entry #key   the reference's key, typed as the `Resolvable` instance's `Operand`
      Needs `Result is Resolvable` in scope, which fixes the key's type.
  entry #apply   resolves the reference to its entity, raising `Reference.Error` if there is none
      Needs `Result is Resolvable` in scope and a `Tactic[Reference.Error]`. Each
      application looks the key up afresh, so a reference made before its entity was added
      to the store resolves once it is there.

entry .Resolvable   a typeclass saying how a key of type `Operand` resolves to an entity of type `Self`
  detail
      `Person is Resolvable by Text` says that a `Text` key identifies a `Person`, which
      field of `Person` is that key (`#field`), and how a key is resolved (`#resolve`). The
      `ref` extension and `Reference#apply` both need one in scope.

      The usual instance is built from a store of values with `Resolvable(store)`:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Person(@primary email: Text, name: Text)

      val people = Set(Person(t"romeo@example.com", t"Romeo"), Person(t"juliet@example.com", t"Juliet"))
      given resolvable: Person is Resolvable by Text = Resolvable(people)

      resolvable.field                          // t"email"
      resolvable.resolve(t"romeo@example.com")   // Person(t"romeo@example.com", t"Romeo")
      ```

      A store of another kind — a database table, a remote service — can implement the
      trait directly.
  refer gnossienne.primary
  refer gnossienne.Reference
  entry .apply   a `Resolvable` which searches a set of values by their `@primary` field
      `Resolvable(store)` needs the entity type to have exactly one field annotated
      `@primary` (so that it is `Annotated by primary`), that field to be selectable as
      `Dereferenceable to Operand`, and the field's type to be `Encodable in Text` (for the
      error message). Both the key's type and the field are inferred from the annotation.

      The store is taken by name and evaluated on every resolution, so a store which
      changes is searched as it is at the moment a reference is applied. The search is a
      linear scan of the set.

      Its result type is `raises Reference.Error`, so building the instance needs a
      `Tactic[Reference.Error]` in scope (or `unsafely(Resolvable(store))`), although
      nothing can fail until a key is resolved.
  entry #Self   the type of the entity a key resolves to
  entry #Operand   the type of the key
  entry #resolve   finds the entity with a given key, raising `Reference.Error` if there is none
  entry #field   the name of the entity's key field, as text

entry .primary   marks the field of a case class which is its key, for `Resolvable(store)`
    An annotation on one parameter of a case class: `case class Person(@primary email:
    Text, name: Text)`. `Resolvable(store)` finds the annotated field through `Annotated by
    primary`, and uses its type as the key type of every `Reference` to the class.

entry .ref   the reference to a value: its key, typed as a `Reference` to the value's type
    An extension on any value whose type is `Resolvable`. It reads the value's key field
    (the one marked `@primary`) and wraps it as a `Reference to Entity`. Nothing is looked
    up, so it needs no tactic; applying the reference later resolves it back.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Person(@primary email: Text, name: Text)

    val juliet = Person(t"juliet@example.com", t"Juliet")
    given Person is Resolvable by Text = unsafely(Resolvable(Set(juliet)))

    juliet.ref.key   // t"juliet@example.com"
    ```
